{# Where this page sits in the site.

   Rendered from the layout, so it lands above whatever each page opens with --
   there is no shared wrapper inside <main> to sit in, since every page opens
   its own. Outside <main> is where a <nav> landmark belongs anyway.

   An empty trail renders nothing at all: that is how home and the booking
   funnel opt out. The funnel shows a step indicator instead, which answers how
   far through the task rather than where in the site.

   The separator is drawn by CSS rather than an <i>, because Font Awesome's JS
   rewrites every <i> into an <svg> and rules that select one stop applying the
   moment it runs. #}
{% set trail = breadcrumbs|default([]) %}

{# Empty or not: a trail is never one crumb, since anything with a name also
   has Home above it. The old `> 1` read as guarding a case that cannot arise. #}
{% if trail is not empty %}
    {# Two options, both for a trail drawn inside a page's own band rather than
       in the strip below the header.

       `on_dark` swaps the colours: the ones here are mixed for a light
       surface, and the palette carries a set for the other case --
       --ink-on-dark and friends -- measured against --brand-ink for this.

       `hide_current` drops the last crumb, because the heading directly below
       a band already says it: "Home > Help > Baggage" above a title reading
       "Baggage" states the page twice. What is left is the path to the page
       rather than the page, which is the job the reference gives an "all
       articles" eyebrow -- and CSS puts a separator after it so the trail
       points into the heading instead of stopping short.

       The structured data below is built from the whole trail either way. It
       describes the page's position to a crawler, and that does not change
       because the last step is drawn as a heading instead of a crumb. #}
    {% set shown = hide_current|default(false) ? trail|filter(crumb => not crumb.current) : trail %}

    <nav class="breadcrumbs{{ on_dark|default(false) ? ' breadcrumbs--on-dark' : '' }}{{ hide_current|default(false) ? ' breadcrumbs--to-heading' : '' }}" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <div class="container">
            <ol class="breadcrumbs__list">
                {% for crumb in shown %}
                    <li class="breadcrumbs__item{{ crumb.current ? ' breadcrumbs__item--current' : '' }}"
                        {{ crumb.current ? 'aria-current="page"' : '' }}>
                        {% if crumb.url %}
                            <a class="breadcrumbs__link" href="{{ crumb.url }}">{{ crumb.label }}</a>
                        {% else %}
                            {{ crumb.label }}
                        {% endif %}
                    </li>
                {% endfor %}
            </ol>
        </div>
    </nav>

    {# The same trail again, for machines. A search result can then show
       Trip Builder > Airlines rather than a bare URL. Built from `trail`, not
       from the path, so it cannot describe a different page than the crumbs
       above it -- and `raw` is safe because structuredData() hex-escapes `<`,
       which is what a label taken from a URL could otherwise close this tag
       with. #}
    {% set jsonld = breadcrumb_jsonld(trail) %}
    {% if jsonld %}
        <script type="application/ld+json">{{ jsonld|raw }}</script>
    {% endif %}
{% endif %}
